<<<
tendency to differentiate (see NOUN & VERB ACCENT) the noun keeps the accent on the last syllable. entente. See FRENCH WORDS. enteric (fever).
===
tendency to differentiate (see ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^) the noun keeps the accent on the last syllable.

**entente.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.

**enteric (fever).**
>>>

<<<
Newspaper readers who have noticed the disappearance of typhoid & the increase of e. may be glad to learn the relation between them.
===
Newspaper readers who have noticed the disappearance of typhoid & the increase of *e.* may be glad to learn the relation between them.
>>>

<<<
‘ Typhoid fever: a specific
===
‘ *Typhoid fever*: a specific
>>>

<<<
now more usually, called enteric fever "—OED.
===
now more usually, called *enteric* fever ’—OED.
>>>

<<<
enterprise, not -ize4 see -ISE)(-IZE.
===
**enterprise,** not *-ize*; see ^^-ISE^^)(^^-IZE^^.
>>>

<<<
entertain. A guest is entertained at a meal, not to it.
===
**entertain.** A guest is entertained *at* a meal, not *to* it.
>>>

<<<
since then e. to has become stock reporters’ English; Dr Page was entertained to a banquet, Mr Lloyd George to a supper, & the members of the French Free Trade League to luncheon & to dinner.
===
since then *e. to* has become stock reporters' English; Dr Page was entertained *to* a banquet, Mr Lloyd George *to* a supper, & the members of the French Free Trade League *to* luncheon & *to* dinner.
>>>

<<<
that the reporters themselves are beyond cure is plain when one of them states that The Chairman suggested that the missing M.P. had been trapped by suffragists, & followed this up by entertaining the waiting audience to a song.
===
that the reporters themselves are beyond cure is plain when one of them states that *The Chairman suggested that the missing M.P. had been trapped by suffragists, & followed this up by entertaining the waiting audience* to a song.
>>>

<<<
enthral(1). Spell enthrall; see -LL-, -L-.
===
**enthral(l).** Spell *enthrall*; see ^^-LL-^^, ^^-L-^^.
>>>

<<<
enthuse. See BACK-FORMATION.
===
**enthuse.** See ^^BACK-FORMATION^^.
>>>

<<<
entire. There is not a single county in the e. of Ireland in which. . . This, which sounds like a foolish modern use comparable with that of VARIOUS, is in fact an old one, become ‘ somewhat rare’ (OED). It may as well become rarer, for all that.
===
**entire.** *There is not a single county in the e. of Ireland in which. . .* This, which sounds like a foolish modern use comparable with that of ^^VARIOUS^^, is in fact an old one, become ‘ somewhat rare ’ (OED). It may as well become rarer, for all that.
>>>

<<<
entitled means having a right (to do something) or a just claim (to some advantage); it does not mean bound (to do) or liable (to a penalty); but it is now being badly misused:— Germany has suffered bitterly, is 141
===
**entitled** means having a right (*to* do something) or a just claim (*to* some advantage); it does not mean bound (*to* do) or liable (*to* a penalty); but it is now being badly misused:—*Germany has suffered bitterly, is*
>>>

<<<


+ suffering bitterly, & Germany is entitled to suffer for what she has done./If these people choose to come here [into court] & will not learn our heathen language, but prefer their gibberish or jargon, I consider they are entitled to pay for it.
===

+ *suffering bitterly, & Germany is* entitled to suffer *for what she has done./If these people choose to come here* [into court] *& will not learn our heathen language, but prefer their gibberish or jargon, I consider they* are entitled to pay for it.
>>>

<<<
entity. The word is one of those regarded by plain people, whether readers or writers, with some alarm & distrust as smacking of philosophy.
===
**entity.** The word is one of those regarded by plain people, whether readers or writers, with some alarm & distrust as smacking of philosophy.
>>>

<<<
which no one shies at; e. is being, & an e. is a being. The first or abstract sense is comparatively rare; e. is better than nonentity means the same as it is better to be than not to be.
===
which no one shies at; *e.* is *being*, & *an e.* is *a being*. The first or abstract sense is comparatively rare; *e. is better than nonentity* means the same as *it is better to be than not to be*.
>>>

<<<
In the second or concrete sense, an e. differs only so far from a being that the latter as used by others than philosophers has come to exclude, while e. includes, any non-sentient or impersonal but actually existing thing;
===
In the second or concrete sense, *an e.* differs only so far from *a being* that the latter as used by others than philosophers has come to exclude, while *e.* includes, any non-sentient or impersonal but actually existing thing;
>>>

