<<<
entrust. Modern idiom allows only two constructions: to e. (a task, a charge, a secret) fo someone; to e. (someone) with a task &c.
===
**entrust.** Modern idiom allows only two constructions: to e. (a task, a charge, a secret) *to* someone; to e. (someone) *with* a task &c.
>>>

<<<
The verb no longer means to put trust in simply (that is to trust, not e.), nor to commission or employ or charge to do (for which those verbs, or again to trust, will serve).
===
The verb no longer means to put trust in simply (that is to *trust*, not e.), nor to commission or employ or charge to do (for which those verbs, or again to *trust*, will serve).
>>>

<<<
The obsolete uses are seen in: King Edward entrusted him implicitly, & invariably acted upon his advice. /By victory the fighting men have achieved what their country has entrusted them to do. See CAST-IRON IDIOM.
===
The obsolete uses are seen in: *King Edward entrusted him implicitly, & invariably acted upon his advice.*/*By victory the fighting men have achieved what their country* has entrusted them to do. See ^^CAST-IRON IDIOM^^.
>>>

<<<
entrust, entwine, entwist, in-. See EM- & IM-.
===
**entrust, entwine, entwist, in-.** See ^^EM-^^ & ^^IM-^^.
>>>

